      <h1>Market Data Methodology</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Market Intelligence · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Every figure published here carries a <strong>source</strong> and a
        <strong>date</strong>. Numbers are collected from primary sources first, cross-checked, and flagged rather than
        silently averaged when sources conflict. No figure is fabricated or extrapolated without saying so.
      </div>

      <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Source type</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1 — Primary</td><td>Government &amp; official statistics</td><td>Customs/export data, national statistics bureaus, trade bodies</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2 — Industry</td><td>Established industry publications</td><td>Trade associations, reputable market research</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3 — Secondary</td><td>News &amp; aggregators</td><td>Used for leads, not final numbers</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why figures conflict — and what we do about it</h2>
      <p>
        Two credible sources can report very different numbers for the same market, and the difference is almost
        always a matter of <strong>scope, definition or date</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Scope</strong> — cells vs packs, new vs recycled, one application vs all applications, value vs volume.</li>
        <li><strong>Definition</strong> — "battery market" can mean manufacturing output, installed capacity, or end-market value.</li>
        <li><strong>Date</strong> — a calendar year vs a rolling period; a forecast vs an actual.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A concrete example: the "China battery market" is quoted at roughly <strong>USD 7 billion</strong> in one scope
        and roughly <strong>USD 50 billion</strong> in another. Both are defensible — they measure different things. The
        disciplined move is to show both with their scope, not to pick one, which is what this site does (see the
        <a href="/china-battery-market/">China battery market</a> report).
      </p>

      <h2>The rules</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Source and date on every figure</strong> — a number without both is not published.</li>
        <li><strong>Conflict is flagged</strong> — when two sources disagree, the disagreement is stated and its likely reason noted, not hidden behind an average.</li>
        <li><strong>No fabricated market size, price or growth rate</strong> — if a figure cannot be sourced, it is omitted.</li>
        <li><strong>Unit and currency are stated</strong> — no silent conversion or false precision.</li>
        <li><strong>Data period and geography are stated</strong> — a "2025" figure says whether it is full-year, YTD, or a forecast.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> Market data is only as trustworthy as its sourcing — a chart with no source is
        decoration, and a sourced number with a visible conflict is the honest version. The useful skill is reading
        the scope, not the headline number.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> Battery-market figures vary wildly between sources because of different definitions and
        dates. The disciplined move is to show the source and the conflict, not to produce a fake-clean single number
        — and the reader who asks "what scope and what date" is the one who actually understands the figure.</p>
